diagnosis_text:
I verified the “return route” from the partner network (pc2: 200.1.1.2) back to you (pc1: 195.11.14.2). The network is operating normally and the reverse path is correctly installed and forwarding.

Evidence:
- End-to-end reachability is clean both ways: pc1→pc2 and pc2→pc1 pings show 0% loss and ~1 ms RTT.
- On router2 (partner side), the return route to your subnet is learned via eBGP and installed: 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1 dev eth0 (proto bgp). On router1, the forward route to 200.1.1.0/24 is via 193.10.11.2 (proto bgp).
- BGP sessions are up on both routers with prefixes exchanged (show ip bgp summary: each side receives 1 prefix and sends 2), confirming dynamic routing is healthy.
- End hosts have correct defaults: pc2 default via 200.1.1.1 (router2), pc1 default via 195.11.14.1 (router1).

Mechanism/Localization: Traffic from pc2 to pc1 returns over router2:eth0 → router1:eth0 → router1:eth1 → pc1, matching the installed routes and confirmed by successful ICMP replies.

Root cause: No network fault found; routing and forwarding for the return path are correct and functioning.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. If the “one-way” issue persists for a specific application, investigate host-level factors (application/service listeners, local firewalls, or port-specific policies) and consider packet captures on pc1 and pc2 during the issue window to isolate non-network causes. 
